    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that fingerprint analogy makes some sense but it's a bit vague. could you explain it more in terms of actual waves? like, if i have a messy, complicated wave, how does the math actually pull apart the individual sine and cosine waves from it? like, what is the actual physical mechanism of the decomposition?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine taking apart a train of waves that represents your complicated signal. In the realm of classical physics, we can decompose a wave train into individual sine waves (acoustic or light waves) using techniques like Fourier analysis. This is made possible by the concept of superposition, where two or more waves can combine in phase (i.e., occur at the same frequency and phase) to produce a resulting wave pattern.",
      "source": "target",
